## Tier 3 — Population / statistical ideas

Facts about the codebase as a population of symbols.

### 9. Family and structural-twin detection

Find sets of symbols with parallel roles: implementations of an interface, members of a union/visitor, symbols with near-identical edge patterns (same callers/callees/types). The raw material for completeness reasoning and for #10.

### 10. Convention anomaly detection

Within a family, compute what most members share and flag the exceptions ("23 of 24 handlers call checkPermission(); this one doesn't"). Dual use: the anomaly may be the bug; the majority pattern is the template for new code. Noisiest idea in the catalog — false conventions are worse than silence; likely built after co-change, which provides tuning ground truth.

### 11. Canonical pattern retrieval

Point the agent at the place the repository already solved an analogous problem, so it reuses the project's convention instead of inventing a locally plausible one. Non-magic version: surface structural analogies and historical footprints (e.g., the existing control-character rendering pair in the Unicode task) rather than semantic similarity.

### 12. Reference locality

Fraction of a symbol's references that stay within its own file/package. Low locality = public contract (edits need propagation); high = implementation detail. Cheap annotation candidate.

### 13. Real module map (clustering vs. directory layout)

Community detection on the reference graph to reveal behavioral modules, compared against the directory tree; the disagreements and the bridge edges between clusters are the payload (the bracket task's decisive seam, delivered without the lucky double-overview). Caveats: boundary stability across algorithm knobs; label clusters only by dominant directory + bridges, never invented names.

### 14. Churn × fan-in hotspots

Join history with structure: heavily-changed, load-bearing symbols as risk/defect-density signals.

