## Tier 4 — Trajectory-shaping ideas (when/how, not what)

Ideas about delivery and timing rather than new analyses. Largely orthogonal to Tiers 1–3.

### 15. Annotation-first delivery

General principle extracted from the session: prefer enriching outputs the agent already requests over adding views it must know to ask for. Pull-based commands aimed at completeness/provenance tend to arrive after the agent has a theory — too late.

### 16. Pre-finalization audit / checkpoint

Before a patch is finalized, automatically check the diff against the facts above: co-change siblings not touched, family members not updated, symmetric pairs left unaligned. The "what did this patch miss?" question as a semantic audit step.

### 17. Next-question recommendations

Map agent situations to the concept that answers them ("where did this value come from?" → provenance; "what must change together?" → siblings) and embed recommendations in existing output, rather than requiring the agent to know the taxonomy.

### 18. Persistent compressed codebase maps

Compute-once, cache, and reuse repo-level artifacts (module map, family index, co-change table) so orientation cost amortizes across tasks.

